l":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Housing and Environment Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-22","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Pension Fund Panel","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Health and Adult Social Services Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Treasury income, described as strategically important to council funding, will decline as interest rates fall.","lead":"If treasury income is material to the council's budget (as it states), a significant and sustained fall in investment returns will reduce its revenue baseline. The council will face harder choices about spending or new borrowing, or it must actively manage\u2026","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council raised \u00a311.8m in new debt for third-party lending arrangements and increased exposure to an unrated housing provider by 155%.","lead":"The council is taking on debt not for its own capital programme but to intermediate lending to third parties. An unrated housing provider adds credit risk to the council's balance sheet. Lenders care about the composition and quality of the council's\u2026","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Capital Financing Requirement jumped 27% in one quarter to \u00a31.32bn, but the council raised no new borrowing to fund it.","lead":"A 27% increase in the council's capital financing requirement is material and suggests either its capital ambitions have expanded significantly or its accounting has shifted. A lender needs to understand which, as it determines future borrowing need and\u2026","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","headline":"Treasury income, described as strategically important to council funding, will decline as interest rates fall.","detail":"The council held \u00a3332.209m in investments earning an average return of 3.916%, outperforming the SONIA overnight benchmark of 3.730% by 0.186%. The Bank of England held rates at 3.75% through June 2026; forecasts predict a fall to 3.50% by September 2027 and further gradual decline. The council explicitly states that income from treasury management 'makes a significant contribution to the resources available to fund the council's strategic objectives', yet this income stream will erode as rates fall. The council acknowledges it is monitoring its borrowing position 'as current market conditions start to shift and interest rates continue to fall'.","category":"income","matters":"If treasury income is material to the council's budget (as it states), a significant and sustained fall in investment returns will reduce its revenue baseline. The council will face harder choices about spending or new borrowing, or it must actively manage its debt position. Lenders care about the stability of revenue streams and how councils absorb financial shocks.","figures":"Investment balance: \u00a3332.209m. Average return Q1 2026/27: 3.916%. SONIA benchmark: 3.730%. Outperformance: 0.186%. Bank rate: 3.75% (current to June 2026). Forecast Bank rate: 3.50% by September 2027.","quote":"Proper and effective management of the Treasury Management function is an essential element of the financial administration process within the council. Income generated from this source makes a significant contribution to the resources available to fund the council's strategic objectives.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","headline":"Council raised \u00a311.8m in new debt for third-party lending arrangements and increased exposure to an unrated housing provider by 155%.","detail":"The council increased its long-term borrowing by \u00a311.825m in Q1 to fund lending arrangements for West London Waste Authority (\u00a311.5m) and Mortlake Crematorium (\u00a30.325m) under Service Level Agreements. Total third-party funds held grew to \u00a367.375m. Separately, the council's capital financing requirement linked to Broadway Living Register Provider\u2014an unrated housing intermediary\u2014jumped from \u00a337.268m to \u00a394.915m, with \u00a324.909m in direct unrated loans to BLRP. This concentrates the council's asset base in entities outside its direct control.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council is taking on debt not for its own capital programme but to intermediate lending to third parties. An unrated housing provider adds credit risk to the council's balance sheet. Lenders care about the composition and quality of the council's assets and the concentration of exposure.","figures":"New borrowing for WLWA and Mortlake: \u00a311.825m. WLWA total exposure: \u00a361.500m. Mortlake total: \u00a35.875m. BLRP direct loans: \u00a324.909m. BLRP CFR: \u00a394.915m (up 155% from \u00a337.268m).","quote":"The council has an agreement with West London Waste Authority (WLWA) and the Board of Mortlake Crematorium to invest funds on behalf of these organisations. The total funds under the arrangement at 30 June 2026 were \u00a367.375m (\u00a361.500m WLWA and \u00a35.875m Mortlake Crematorium).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Audit Committee - Treasury Management Q1 2026-2027 -New Template - Draft v2","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27180/27180.pdf","headline":"Capital Financing Requirement jumped 27% in one quarter to \u00a31.32bn, but the council raised no new borrowing to fund it.","detail":"The Capital Financing Requirement (the council's underlying need to borrow to fund capital expenditure) increased from \u00a31,043.462m to \u00a31,322.295m between 31 March and 30 June 2026. The General Fund element alone jumped by \u00a3162.323m; the BLRP (housing intermediary) element by \u00a357.647m. Yet the council raised no new long-term borrowing for capital expenditure in the quarter and its total borrowing remains at \u00a3853.798m, leaving a gap of \u00a3468.5m. The paper attributes lower financing costs to 'slippage on the capital programme' but does not explain why the CFR rose so sharply.","category":"capital","matters":"A 27% increase in the council's capital financing requirement is material and suggests either its capital ambitions have expanded significantly or its accounting has shifted. A lender needs to understand which, as it determines future borrowing need and refinancing risk.","figures":"CFR 30 June 2026: \u00a31,322.295m. Actual borrowing: \u00a3853.798m. Variance: \u00a3468.497m. General Fund CFR increased \u00a3162.323m. BLRP loans/equity CFR increased \u00a357.647m (+155%).","quote":"The council's actual borrowing stood at \u00a3853.798m which is below the CFR of \u00a31,322.295m. The council's borrowing requirements are financed through both external and internal borrowing, although no external borrowing was raised during the financial year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 1_LB Ealing_2025.26_Forvis Mazars_Sept 26_Audit Progress Report","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27183/27183.pdf","headline":"Valid public objection to draft 2025/26 accounts accepted; procedures underway to determine outcome","detail":"During the public inspection period, the auditor received three pieces of correspondence from members of the public. One local elector submitted an objection to the draft 2025/26 accounts; after completing the required NAO eligibility checks, this objection was accepted as valid. The auditor is planning procedures to determine the nature and outcome of the objection.","category":"governance","matters":"A valid public objection to accounts signals that someone has identified a potential material error or concern with the accounts. Public objections are unusual and indicate potential reliability or disclosure issues requiring investigation.","figures":"Three items of public correspondence received; one accepted as valid objection","quote":"one local elector made an objection to the draft 2025/26 accounts. We completed the eligibility checks as required by the National Audit Office guidance and accepted the objection as valid.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 1_LB Ealing_2025.26_Forvis Mazars_Sept 26_Audit Progress Report","paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s27183/27183.pdf","headline":"Audit progress slower than planned; scope revised to focus on material balances only rather than complete planned testing","detail":"Since the July 2026 audit planning meeting, rebuilding assurance work and 2025/26 audit procedures have progressed more slowly than anticipated. The auditor and council have agreed a revised approach that narrows scope to testing a sample of balances from prior disclaimed years plus material assets and liabilities in 2025/26, rather than completing all originally planned procedures. Sample requests for material items were planned for issue in September 2026, with testing to commence in late October.","category":"audit","matters":"Slower audit progress and reduced scope indicate the council may lack the financial capability or resource capacity to support full audit procedures. This affects timeline for finalised accounts and indicates potential resource constraints at the council.","figures":"Testing to commence late October 2026; originally expected to be further advanced by September 2026","quote":"Since the July 2026 meeting whilst we have made some progress in commencing rebuilding assurance work and aspects of the 2025/26audit, progress has not been at the pace needed.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-09"},"name":"Ealing","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Business rates collection 2.42% behind target, and declining 2.41% year-on-year","lead":"Year-on-year business rates collection decline, beyond government billing delays, signals underlying income risk affecting cash flow and medium-term revenue.","also":["12 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Children's Services Scrutiny Panel on 2026-09-22","n_findings":13,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report Quarter 1 202627 FINAL","top_paper_url":"https://ealing.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s26905/26905.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":83,"news_drafts_total":3}}